The focus motor no longer poisons itself when suspending

THE SYMPTOM
-----------
With patch 0101 applied, the driver binds, the node appears and the entity is in
the graph:

    /dev/v4l-subdev17 -> dw9807 12-000c
    - entity 173: dw9807 12-000c (0 pad, 0 link)

But the node CANNOT BE OPENED, not even with the sensor streaming:

    Failed to open /dev/v4l-subdev17: Invalid argument
    open('/dev/v4l-subdev17') -> errno 22

And nothing appears in dmesg when trying. There is only one line, from boot:

    [   12.486398] dw9807 12-000c: I2C write CTL fail ret = -110

That silence is the important clue. If the problem were the bus, the failure
would be -110 (timeout) or -6, and CCI would leave its "master 0 queue 0
timeout". A mute 22 is not an i2c failure: it is the subsystem refusing before
reaching the chip.

THE CAUSE
---------
The whole chain fits in four lines of kernel.

`dw9807_open()` does nothing but wake the chip up:

    static int dw9807_open(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, struct v4l2_subdev_fh *fh)
    {
    	return pm_runtime_resume_and_get(sd->dev);
    }

`rpm_resume()` starts by rejecting any device whose power state is broken, and
that is the 22:

    if (dev->power.runtime_error) { retval = -EINVAL; goto out; }

And `rpm_callback()` stores in `runtime_error` whatever the power function
returns, be it for sleeping or for waking:

    retval = __rpm_callback(cb, dev);
    dev->power.runtime_error = retval;

So all that is missing is whoever breaks it. It is `dw9807_vcm_suspend()`, which
powers the chip down over i2c and returns the error if it cannot:

    ret = i2c_master_send(client, tx_data, sizeof(tx_data));
    if (ret < 0) {
    	dev_err(&client->dev, "I2C write CTL fail ret = %d\n", ret);
    	return ret;
    }

On this board that power-down ALWAYS fails when it comes on its own, because the
sensor's CCI bus only lives while the sensor is streaming -a restriction already
documented in patch 0101-. The driver suspends a few seconds after boot, the
write gives -110, and from that instant on the state stays at `error` and every
open returns 22. Forever, even if the bus works perfectly afterwards.

It is permanent poisoning caused by a transient failure, and on top of that from
the least important operation the driver performs.

THE FIX
-------
Do not propagate the failure out of the power functions. It is still logged, but
success is returned, which is the only thing that keeps `runtime_error` from
getting stuck.

It is the right thing in both directions:

  - When SLEEPING, not being able to power the chip down is not a failure that
    needs reporting upwards. The rail stays on anyway (`regulator-always-on` in
    0101), the actuator's idle consumption is negligible, and there is nothing to
    retry.

  - When WAKING, likewise: the one that wants to move the lens is the
    `focus_absolute` write, and that one DOES return its error to whoever made
    it. So an attempt to focus with the camera stopped still gives EIO, visible
    and in the right place. What is avoided is that failure killing the whole
    node for all future uses.

In short: the error stops being fatal and goes back to being what it was, a
warning.

HOW IT WAS CHECKED
------------------
By hand, before touching the driver, the state was cleared by rebinding it
through sysfs with the sensor streaming:

    power state BEFORE:  error
    (unbind + bind with a capture running)
    power state AFTER:   suspended

And the node then opened first time, with its control:

    focus_absolute 0x009a090a (int) : min=0 max=1023 step=1 default=0

With positions going in and the chip's high register following them:

    requested 300 -> v4l2 says 300, the chip says 0x01 ...
    requested 600 -> v4l2 says 600, the chip says 0x02 ...
    requested 900 -> v4l2 says 900, the chip says 0x03 ...

This patch makes that manual rebind unnecessary on every boot.

--- a/drivers/media/i2c/dw9807-vcm.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/dw9807-vcm.c
@@ -249,11 +249,17 @@
 
 	/* Power down */
 	ret = i2c_master_send(client, tx_data, sizeof(tx_data));
-	if (ret < 0) {
+	if (ret < 0)
 		dev_err(&client->dev, "I2C write CTL fail ret = %d\n", ret);
-		return ret;
-	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The error is not propagated. On boards where the actuator bus only lives
+	 * while the sensor transmits, this power-off always fails when it comes
+	 * alone, and rpm_callback() would store the error in dev->power.runtime_error;
+	 * from then on rpm_resume() returns -EINVAL and the node can never be
+	 * opened again. Not being able to power the chip off does not deserve that: what
+	 * really needs the bus is the position write, and that one does report.
+	 */
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -273,10 +279,10 @@
 
 	/* Power on */
 	ret = i2c_master_send(client, tx_data, sizeof(tx_data));
-	if (ret < 0) {
+	if (ret < 0)
 		dev_err(&client->dev, "I2C write CTL fail ret = %d\n", ret);
-		return ret;
-	}
+
+	/* Same reason as in suspend: do not poison the power state. */
 
 	for (val = dw9807_dev->current_val % DW9807_CTRL_STEPS;
 	     val < dw9807_dev->current_val + DW9807_CTRL_STEPS - 1;
